<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samuel Johnson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1709">1709</a>–<a href="/years/1784">1784</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Dr Johnson</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samuel Johnson was an English writer, critic and lexicographer. His Dictionary of the English Language of 1755, compiled largely alone over nine years, was the most influential English dictionary until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> English Dictionary. He also wrote poetry, essays, the novella Rasselas and the Lives of the Poets, and his friend James Boswell's Life of Johnson made him one of the best-known figures of his age.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samuel-johnson.jpg" alt="Samuel Johnson" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joshua Reynolds · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samuel_Johnson_by_Joshua_Reynolds.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Samuel Johnson was born on 18 September 1709 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lichfield">Lichfield</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staffordshire">Staffordshire</a>, above the bookshop of his father Michael Johnson. His mother Sarah was 40, an unusually late age for a first birth, and the infant did not cry, so the family feared for his life and had him baptised at once. He attended Pembroke College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, until lack of money forced him to leave. His father fell deeply into debt and died in December 1731, when Johnson was twenty-two. Johnson was turned down for a post as usher at Stourbridge Grammar School because he had no degree. After working as a teacher he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and began writing for The Gentleman's Magazine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His early works include the poem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> (1738), the Life of Mr Richard Savage (1744) and the poem The Vanity of Human Wishes (1749). In 1746 a group of publishers approached him to make an authoritative dictionary, and a contract with William Strahan and associates, worth 1,500 guineas, was signed on 18 June. Johnson said the work would take three years, remarking that the Académie Française had used forty scholars for forty years. A Dictionary of the English Language appeared in 1755. Critics included Thomas Babington Macaulay, who called Johnson a wretched etymologist, while the biographer Walter Jackson Bate called it one of the most notable single achievements of scholarship.</p><p>Johnson then wrote the novella The History of Rasselas, Prince of Abissinia (1759) and edited The Plays of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-shakespeare">William Shakespeare</a> (1765). In 1763 he befriended James Boswell, and in August 1773 the two set out on a journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, which Johnson described in A Journey to the Western Islands of Scotland (1775). There he entered the dispute over James Macpherson's Ossian poems, arguing that they could not be translations of ancient Gaelic writing because nothing had been written in that language in those times. Near the end of his life he wrote Lives of the Most Eminent English Poets (1779–1781), biographies and critical appraisals of 52 poets.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Johnson suffered from scrofula, gout, deafness in one ear and blindness in one eye, and from long periods of depression. His tics and gestures, closely recorded by Boswell, have led to a posthumous diagnosis of Tourette syndrome. He died on the evening of 13 December 1784, aged 75, and is buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>. In his criticism he preferred contemporary language and was suspicious of Milton's blank verse and obscure allusions. His Dictionary was pre-eminent until the Oxford English Dictionary appeared some 150 years later, and he changed the course of biography; Boswell's Life of Samuel Johnson (1791) is described by Bate as the best-known single work of biographical art in the whole of literature.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samuel Johnson” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…after the union: David Hume, Adam Smith, whose Wealth of Nations appeared in 1776, and Adam Ferguson shaped modern philosophy, economics and social science, while <b>Samuel Johnson</b>’s Dictionary (1755) and the novels of Daniel Defoe, Samuel Richardson and Henry Fielding helped establish modern English prose and the novel.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Samuel Johnson</b>'s Dictionary of the English Language appeared after nine years' work, defining some 42,000 words with literary quotations.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-15">15 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lichfield">Lichfield</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.68,-1.83&amp;year=1757" aria-label="Lichfield on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4509 347 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Samuel Johnson"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4959 617h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4959 617h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.68,-1.83&amp;year=1757">What was here in 1757? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Why, Sir, you find no man, at all intellectual, who is willing to leave London. No, Sir, when a man is tired of London, he is tired of life; for there is in London all that life can afford.&#8221;</p><footer>In conversation, 20 September 1777 (Boswell, Life of Johnson) &#183; original &#183; Boswell's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1564/pg1564-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Is not a Patron, my Lord, one who looks with unconcern on a man struggling for life in the water, and, when he has reached ground, encumbers him with help?&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Lord Chesterfield, February 1755 &#183; original &#183; as printed in Boswell's Life of Johnson &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1564/pg1564-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Human life is everywhere a state in which much is to be endured and little to be enjoyed.&#8221;</p><footer>Rasselas (1759), chapter 11 &#183; original &#183; spoken by the poet Imlac &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/652/pg652-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Patriotism is the last refuge of a scoundrel.&#8221;</p><footer>In conversation, 7 April 1775 (Boswell, Life of Johnson) &#183; original &#183; Boswell's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1564/pg1564-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Depend upon it, Sir, when a man knows he is to be hanged in a fortnight, it concentrates his mind wonderfully.&#8221;</p><footer>In conversation, 19 September 1777 (Boswell, Life of Johnson) &#183; original &#183; Boswell's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1564/pg1564-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;No man but a blockhead ever wrote, except for money.&#8221;</p><footer>In conversation, 5 April 1776 (Boswell, Life of Johnson) &#183; original &#183; Boswell's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1564/pg1564-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
